(Program generator: emits random valid Vox source text.

 There is no AST - rules append rendered source directly into gen_out.
 Names are v1..vN in declaration order, so every reference is in scope
 by construction. Buffers (bN), lists (lN), thing instances (iN),
 maps (mN), value locals (yN), floats (dN), texts (sN), function-call
 scratch locals (cN), grid loop variables (eN), While counters (uN) and
 Repeat counters (rN) each get their own counter and are always
 self-contained within the one statement that declares them - they are
 never referenced by a later, unrelated statement, so nothing about
 Vox's per-branch declaration visibility can bite them.

 Nesting (loops, ifs) is depth-capped at 3 and threaded through two
 precomputed values on every recursive call: `depth` (nesting budget
 remaining, counted down to 0) and `indent` (the cosmetic prefix for
 this level's lines, two spaces deeper each level). A block's body is
 generated by 'gen body', which is the one place note 6b's trap is
 handled: it always ends with a plain leaf statement, so the block's
 own period reliably closes it even when the body's other action was
 itself a nested loop or if.)

Create a buffer called gen_out.

a number called gen_vars is 0.

a number called gen_args is 0.

a number called gen_floats is 0.

a number called gen_texts is 0.

To 'gen emit' with a text called s.
    a buffer called piece is "{s}".
    append piece to gen_out.

To 'gen new var'.
    Set gen_vars to gen_vars add 1.
    a text called v is 'gen extreme number'.
    a text called line is "a number called v{gen_vars} is {v}.\n".
    'gen emit' of line.
    Return a number, gen_vars.

(Aggressive magnitudes. Josj, 2026-08-20: "instead of always setting
 values to things like 32.8 I wanna see 1243626351836374761.1224435542121323
 ... I wanna make the compiler AND the runtime cry." A generator whose
 every literal is a tame three-digit number never reaches a boundary, so
 it never finds a boundary bug. These two helpers replace `'rng below' of
 1000` wherever a literal's magnitude is free to vary.

 Every value returned is LEGAL Vox that should compile and run - the
 invariant is untouched. i64's exact limits are included deliberately:
 they are the values a saturating or wrapping conversion gets wrong, and
 they are exactly where the compiler stops being able to be lazy.)
(Josj, 2026-08-20, on seeing literal diversity FALL from 0.42 to 0.25:
 "I was giving an example of a big number." Quite. The first version of
 these tables hardcoded his example literal, so every program shared the
 same handful of constants - wide magnitudes, no entropy. These build a
 literal digit by digit instead: random length, random digits. The exact
 i64 boundaries keep a small fixed weight because they are genuinely
 special - they are what found compiler bugs #34 and #35, and a uniform
 draw would land on them essentially never.

 At most 18 digits, so a generated integer literal stays inside i64 by
 construction: whether an over-wide LITERAL is a compile error or a
 silent wrap is a question worth asking deliberately one day, not one to
 trip over while testing something else.)

To 'gen random digits' with a number called 'how many'.
    a number called 'the first digit' is 'rng below' of 9 add 1.
    a text called digits is "{'the first digit'}".
    a number called made is 1.
    While made is less than 'how many',
        a number called d is 'rng below' of 10,
        Set digits to "{digits}{d}",
        Set made to made add 1.
    Return a text, digits.

To 'gen extreme number'.
    a number called pick is 'rng below' of 10.
    If pick is 0 then,
        Return a text, "0".
    If pick is 1 then,
        Return a text, "9223372036854775807".
    If pick is 2 then,
        Return a text, "2147483648".
    If pick is 3 then,
        a number called 'a short length' is 'rng below' of 3 add 1,
        a text called 'a small number' is 'gen random digits' of 'a short length',
        Return a text, 'a small number'.
    a number called 'the length' is 'rng below' of 18 add 1.
    a text called 'the number' is 'gen random digits' of 'the length'.
    Return a text, 'the number'.

(Floats get their own extremes: a long mantissa the double cannot hold
 exactly (rounding is correct behaviour and must not be reported as a
 finding - only a crash or a wildly wrong magnitude is), a value beyond
 2^63 where an i64 route through the parser would show itself, and the
 subnormal end. The very literal Josj asked for is in the table.)

To 'gen extreme float'.
    a number called pick is 'rng below' of 10.
    If pick is 0 then,
        Return a text, "0.000000000000000000001".
    If pick is 1 then,
        Return a text, "9223372036854775807.5".
    a number called 'the whole length' is 'rng below' of 18 add 1.
    a number called 'the fraction length' is 'rng below' of 20 add 1.
    a text called 'the whole part' is 'gen random digits' of 'the whole length'.
    a text called 'the fraction part' is 'gen random digits' of 'the fraction length'.
    Return a text, "{'the whole part'}.{'the fraction part'}".

To 'gen var ref'.
    If gen_vars is 0 then,
        Return a text, "0".
    a number called 'the pick' is 'rng below' of gen_vars.
    a number called 'the chosen variable' is 'the pick' add 1.
    a text called 'the reference' is "v{'the chosen variable'}".
    Return a text, 'the reference'.

To 'gen expr'.
    a text called 'the left operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called 'the right operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a number called 'the operator choice' is 'rng below' of 5.
    If 'the operator choice' is 0 then,
        a text called 'the sum' is "{'the left operand'} add {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the sum'.
    If 'the operator choice' is 1 then,
        a text called 'the difference' is "{'the left operand'} minus {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the difference'.
    If 'the operator choice' is 2 then,
        a text called 'the product' is "{'the left operand'} multiply {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the product'.
    If 'the operator choice' is 3 then,
        a text called 'the times product' is "{'the left operand'} times {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the times product'.
    a text called 'the plain reference' is "{'the left operand'}".
    Return a text, 'the plain reference'.

(Same four shapes as 'gen expr', minus `times` - hand-reduced: `append v1
 times v2 to l1.` fails to parse ("Expected 'to' after value in append
 statement"), while `append v1 multiply v2 to l1.` compiles, and `times`
 works everywhere else (Print, Set, declarations) including as the
 alternate spelling of `multiply` CHANGELOG 0.4.4 introduced - append's
 own value grammar is the one place that alias does not reach. Reported
 separately as a genuine finding, not tuned around; this is the
 generator-side fix so 'gen leaf butif append', the only caller that
 feeds an expression into an append statement's value, never emits the
 illegal form.)

To 'gen append expr'.
    a text called 'the left operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called 'the right operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a number called 'the operator choice' is 'rng below' of 4.
    If 'the operator choice' is 0 then,
        a text called 'the sum' is "{'the left operand'} add {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the sum'.
    If 'the operator choice' is 1 then,
        a text called 'the difference' is "{'the left operand'} minus {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the difference'.
    If 'the operator choice' is 2 then,
        a text called 'the product' is "{'the left operand'} multiply {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the product'.
    a text called 'the plain reference' is "{'the left operand'}".
    Return a text, 'the plain reference'.

(`a` is a reserved word, which is why the operands carry their full
 names.)

(A boolean condition, for the positions where one is legal - an If's own
 `then`/but-if clause, a but-if branch's own condition. Comparisons and
 property checks are grammatically confined to exactly that position in
 0.4.5 (hand-verified: `Print x is greater than y.` and even
 `a boolean called c is x is greater than y.` both fail to parse - "is
 greater than" is not a first-class expression the way `add`/`multiply`
 are, so it must never be handed to 'gen expr's callers). Covers the
 full comparison table plus `modulo ... is equal to 0` and an and/or
 join of two conditions - the operator-matrix gap this leaf-space
 exists to close.)

To 'gen condition'.
    a text called 'the left operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called 'the right operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a number called 'the comparison choice' is 'rng below' of 6.
    a text called 'the comparison' is "".
    If 'the comparison choice' is 0 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "{'the left operand'} is greater than {'the right operand'}".
    If 'the comparison choice' is 1 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "{'the left operand'} is less than {'the right operand'}".
    If 'the comparison choice' is 2 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "{'the left operand'} is equal to {'the right operand'}".
    If 'the comparison choice' is 3 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "{'the left operand'} is not equal to {'the right operand'}".
    If 'the comparison choice' is 4 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "{'the left operand'} is greater than or equal to {'the right operand'}".
    If 'the comparison choice' is 5 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "{'the left operand'} is less than or equal to {'the right operand'}".
    a number called 'the modulus' is 'rng below' of 5 add 2.
    a text called 'the modulo check' is "{'the left operand'} modulo {'the modulus'} is equal to 0".
    a number called 'the joiner choice' is 'rng below' of 3.
    If 'the joiner choice' is 0 then,
        Return a text, 'the comparison'.
    If 'the joiner choice' is 1 then,
        a text called 'the and form' is "{'the comparison'} and {'the modulo check'}",
        Return a text, 'the and form'.
    a text called 'the or form' is "{'the comparison'} or {'the modulo check'}".
    Return a text, 'the or form'.

(gen line piece: the punctuation a body line needs depends entirely on
 whether it is nested inside an already-open clause. Nested, every
 line but the last continues that clause's action list with a comma;
 unnested (true top level), commas do not parse at all outside a
 clause, so every line is its own period-terminated sentence. The
 comma is what keeps a name declared on one line usable on the next -
 without it, the period would close the enclosing clause immediately,
 exactly the trap note 6b describes.)

To 'gen line piece' with a text called indent and a text called content and a boolean called continues.
    If continues then,
        a text called piece is "{indent}{content},\n",
        Return a text, piece.
    a text called piece2 is "{indent}{content}.\n".
    Return a text, piece2.

To 'gen join lines' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested and a list called 'the lines'.
    a number called 'the count' is 'the lines's length.
    a number called 'the position' is 1.
    While 'the position' is less than 'the count',
        a text called 'the line' is element 'the position' of 'the lines',
        a text called piece is 'gen line piece' of indent and 'the line' and nested,
        'gen emit' of piece,
        Set 'the position' to 'the position' add 1.
    a text called 'the last line' is element 'the count' of 'the lines'.
    a text called 'final piece' is 'gen line piece' of indent and 'the last line' and false.
    'gen emit' of 'final piece'.

(Deep expressions and the full operator matrix. Plan 323 lists this as
 "shallow, not absent": 'gen expr' emits exactly two operands and one of
 five operators, never nested, and 'gen leaf deep arithmetic' emits ONE
 hardcoded three-operand template. So the compiler's precedence and
 grouping rules — the richest part of its expression handling — were
 exercised by two fixed shapes.

 This builds an expression ITERATIVELY rather than recursively: start
 from a single operand and repeatedly wrap it, either as
 `{so far OP operand}` or as `{operand OP so far}`. Arbitrary depth, no
 recursion needed, and mixing the two wrap directions means the tree is
 not uniformly left-leaning — which is the point, since a
 left-associative bug and a right-associative one look identical under a
 left-only generator.

 Grouping in Vox is `{ }`, and in a format string a literal brace is
 doubled — hence the `{{` / `}}` below. Verified by hand against 0.4.6:
 `20 add 6 multiply 3` is 38 (multiply binds tighter),
 `{20 add 6} multiply 3` is 78, `20 minus 6 minus 3` is 11
 (left-associative), and `{{20 add 6} multiply 3} modulo 7` is 1.

 divide and modulo are included with a NONZERO literal divisor. Not
 because zero would be illegal — `x divide 0` is legal Vox that sets the
 error flag and yields 0, verified — but because an unguarded random
 divisor would make a large fraction of programs into division-by-zero
 tests rather than precedence tests. The zero case deserves its own
 leaf; it is not this one's job.)
(`grouped` false builds the SAME expression without Vox's `{ }` grouping
 punctuation, which is what a format string needs. Hand-verified against
 0.4.6 before this parameter existed: inside a format string, `{{` is the
 documented literal-brace ESCAPE (LANGUAGE.md "Escape Sequences"), so a
 grouped expression handed to an interpolation slot does not group - it
 renders as literal text. `Print "{{{x add y} multiply z}}"` prints
 `{13 multiply z}`, not `52`. That is the escape rule working as
 documented, not a defect, and it is why 'gen leaf format expression'
 asks for the flat form rather than a second helper: one operator table,
 two renderings. A flat chain still exercises precedence for real -
 `x add y multiply z` is 22, not 52 - it just cannot override it.)

To 'gen deep expr' with a number called depth and a boolean called grouped.
    a text called 'the expression' is 'gen var ref'.
    a number called i is 0.
    While i is less than depth,
        a number called 'the operator choice' is 'rng below' of 6,
        a text called op is "add",
        If 'the operator choice' is 1 then,
            Set op to "minus".
        If 'the operator choice' is 2 then,
            Set op to "multiply".
        If 'the operator choice' is 3 then,
            Set op to "times".
        If 'the operator choice' is 4 then,
            Set op to "divide".
        If 'the operator choice' is 5 then,
            Set op to "modulo".
        a text called operand is 'gen var ref',
        If 'the operator choice' is greater than 3 then,
            a number called 'the safe divisor' is 'rng below' of 9 add 2,
            Set operand to "{'the safe divisor'}".
        a number called 'the side' is 'rng below' of 2,
        a text called 'the left side' is 'the expression',
        a text called 'the right side' is operand,
        If 'the side' is 1 then,
            Set 'the left side' to operand,
            Set 'the right side' to 'the expression'.
        If grouped then,
            Set 'the expression' to "{{{'the left side'} {op} {'the right side'}}}".
        If grouped is false then,
            Set 'the expression' to "{'the left side'} {op} {'the right side'}".
        Set i to i add 1.
    Return a text, 'the expression'.

To 'gen leaf deep expression' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_texts to gen_texts add 1.
    a number called n is gen_texts.
    a number called depth is 'rng below' of 8 add 2.
    a text called e is 'gen deep expr' of depth and true.
    a text called decl is "a number called dx{n} is {e}".
    a text called 'print line' is "Print dx{n}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'print line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf print' with a text called indent.
    a text called e is 'gen expr'.
    a text called line is "{indent}Print {e}.\n".
    'gen emit' of line.

(The one leaf that uses a variable reference as an assignment TARGET
 rather than as a value. With an empty variable pool 'gen var ref'
 returns the literal "0" — fine as a value, invalid as a target, and
 `Set 0 to 0 times 0.` is illegal Vox. That is what made the degenerate
 no-globals program unreachable (seed 300019).

 The cure is local, not global: declare a fresh number in THIS block, at
 THIS indent, immediately before assigning to it. A top-level
 declaration emitted from here would be wrong instead — this leaf can be
 selected inside an If or loop body, and a name declared on a single
 conditional path is out of scope afterwards (bug #25's family), which
 the compiler is right to reject.)

To 'gen leaf assign' with a text called indent.
    a text called e is 'gen expr'.
    (ONE statement, not two. An earlier version emitted a declaration and
     then a separate `Set`, each ending in a period - and a period closes
     the innermost open clause, so the first one closed the enclosing If
     or loop early and every comma-continued line after it was orphaned
     at the wrong level. That produced `Expected a statement, got Comma`
     on 3 of 600 programs (seeds 7000007 and 7000267 among them).

     A declaration takes an initializer, so the separate assignment was
     never needed: one statement, one period, safe wherever a single
     leaf is safe. This leaf emits directly rather than through
     'gen join lines' and so has no `nested` flag to consult - which is
     exactly why it must emit a single statement and nothing more.)
    If gen_vars is 0 then,
        Set gen_texts to gen_texts add 1,
        a text called 'the fresh declaration' is "{indent}a number called an{gen_texts} is {e}.\n",
        'gen emit' of 'the fresh declaration',
        Return a boolean, false.
    a text called target is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called line is "{indent}Set {target} to {e}.\n".
    'gen emit' of line.
    Return a boolean, false.

(Function calls: the generated program always defines f1/f2/f3 (0, 1,
 and 2 number parameters) in its prelude, so a call site only has to
 pick one and precompute its arguments into fresh locals - language
 note 3.)

To 'gen call zero' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called line is "Print f1".
    a list called 'the lines' is [line].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen call one' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_args to gen_args add 1.
    a number called 'arg value' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called 'arg decl' is "a number called c{gen_args} is {'arg value'}".
    a text called 'call line' is "Print f2 of c{gen_args}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['arg decl', 'call line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen call two' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_args to gen_args add 1.
    a number called 'first name' is gen_args.
    a number called 'first value' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called 'first decl' is "a number called c{'first name'} is {'first value'}".
    Set gen_args to gen_args add 1.
    a number called 'second name' is gen_args.
    a number called 'second value' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called 'second decl' is "a number called c{'second name'} is {'second value'}".
    a text called 'call line' is "Print f3 of c{'first name'} and c{'second name'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['first decl', 'second decl', 'call line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Floats: decimal literals, float multiply and divide, and a mixed
 float/int expression - the whole float codegen path, unfuzzed until
 now (finding 04). The divisor's whole part is drawn from 1 upward so it
 is never 0.0 by construction - proving the divide codegen path is the
 goal, not proving the language's own division-by-zero behaviour, which
 is a different question this generator does not ask.)

To 'gen leaf float arithmetic' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_floats to gen_floats add 1.
    a number called 'first name' is gen_floats.
    a text called 'first literal' is 'gen extreme float'.
    Set gen_floats to gen_floats add 1.
    a number called 'second name' is gen_floats.
    a number called 'second whole' is 'rng below' of 20 add 1.
    a number called 'second frac' is 'rng below' of 100.
    (The divisor stays modest and strictly nonzero on purpose: this leaf
     divides by it, and a zero or subnormal divisor would make every
     program a division-by-zero test rather than a magnitude test. The
     dividend carries the extremes instead.)
    Set gen_floats to gen_floats add 1.
    a number called 'product name' is gen_floats.
    Set gen_floats to gen_floats add 1.
    a number called 'quotient name' is gen_floats.
    Set gen_floats to gen_floats add 1.
    a number called 'mixed name' is gen_floats.
    a text called 'first decl' is "a float called d{'first name'} is {'first literal'}".
    a text called 'second decl' is "a float called d{'second name'} is {'second whole'}.{'second frac'}".
    a text called 'product decl' is "a float called d{'product name'} is d{'first name'} multiply d{'second name'}".
    a text called 'print product' is "Print d{'product name'}".
    a text called 'quotient decl' is "a float called d{'quotient name'} is d{'first name'} divide d{'second name'}".
    a text called 'print quotient' is "Print d{'quotient name'}".
    a text called 'the int operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called 'mixed decl' is "a float called d{'mixed name'} is d{'first name'} add {'the int operand'}".
    a text called 'print mixed' is "Print d{'mixed name'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['first decl', 'second decl', 'product decl', 'print product', 'quotient decl', 'print quotient', 'mixed decl', 'print mixed'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf call' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'call kind' is 'rng below' of 4.
    If 'call kind' is 0 then,
        'gen call zero' of indent and nested.
    If 'call kind' is 1 then,
        'gen call one' of indent and nested.
    If 'call kind' is 2 then,
        'gen call two' of indent and nested.
    If 'call kind' is 3 then,
        'gen call grid' of indent and nested.

(Same three call shapes, minus the grid - kind 19's job is standing in
 for kind 6 wherever the grid's `each ... and each ...` clause would be
 unsafe as a block closer (see 'gen statement's avoid-onerror remap),
 without losing zero/one/two's coverage in that position too.)

To 'gen leaf call safe' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'call kind' is 'rng below' of 3.
    If 'call kind' is 0 then,
        'gen call zero' of indent and nested.
    If 'call kind' is 1 then,
        'gen call one' of indent and nested.
    If 'call kind' is 2 then,
        'gen call two' of indent and nested.

(Deep expressions and the operator matrix: modulo, precedence (`*`/`%`
 binding tighter than `+`), and `{...}` grouping to override it - Vox's
 own grouping punctuation, since real parentheses are comments (see
 'Comments'). Hand-verified: `{v1 add v2} multiply v3 modulo 4` and
 nested `{{...} multiply ...} modulo ...` both compile and evaluate with
 the documented precedence. A plain Print of an arithmetic expression
 never opens a clause, so this is a safe closer - unlike 'gen leaf rich
 condition' below, nothing here is a comparison, which 0.4.5 confines to
 an If/but-if condition position and refuses everywhere else (see 'gen
 condition's own comment).)

To 'gen leaf deep arithmetic' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the left operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called 'the right operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called 'the third operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a number called 'the modulus' is 'rng below' of 5 add 2.
    a text called 'the deep expr' is "{{{'the left operand'} add {'the right operand'}}} multiply {'the third operand'} modulo {'the modulus'}".
    a text called line is "Print {'the deep expr'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [line].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(The comparison/and-or side of the operator matrix, in the one position
 0.4.5 allows it: an If condition. 'gen condition' already covers the
 full comparison table plus modulo plus and/or; this leaf is what
 actually exercises it, printing one of two expressions depending on
 which branch fires. Hand-verified UNSAFE as a block's mandatory closer
 - an If/Otherwise sole-closer traps the next statement the same way a
 nested loop does (both hand-reduced against 0.4.5) - so this remaps to
 1 under avoid-onerror and stays out of 'gen any leaf', same as 'gen
 if' itself is never used directly as a closer.)

To 'gen leaf rich condition' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called condition is 'gen condition'.
    a text called 'the yes branch' is 'gen expr'.
    a text called 'the no branch' is 'gen expr'.
    a text called line is "If {condition} then, Print {'the yes branch'}. Otherwise, Print {'the no branch'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [line].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Kind-number reservation for T3+ parallel work. 0-99 is the existing
 draw ('gen dispatch leaf' below); each block reserves a span for new
 leaves so two workers extending different surfaces never pick the same
 number. Do not renumber an existing leaf to fit this scheme - these are
 reservations for FUTURE leaves only.
   buffers      100-119
   files        120-139
   collections  140-159
   text         160-179
   things       180-199
   flow         200-219
   misc         220-239)

To 'gen dispatch leaf' with a number called kind and a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    If kind is 0 then,
        'gen leaf print' of indent.
    If kind is 1 then,
        'gen leaf assign' of indent.
    If kind is 2 then,
        'gen leaf buffer inrange' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 3 then,
        'gen leaf buffer oob' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 4 then,
        'gen leaf list inrange' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 5 then,
        'gen leaf list oob' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 6 then,
        'gen leaf call' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 7 then,
        'gen leaf thing' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 8 then,
        'gen leaf thing copy' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 9 then,
        'gen leaf map inrange' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 10 then,
        'gen leaf map oob' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 11 then,
        'gen leaf value roundtrip' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 12 then,
        'gen leaf float arithmetic' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 13 then,
        'gen leaf thing member' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 14 then,
        'gen leaf list mixed' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 15 then,
        'gen leaf text value' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 19 then,
        'gen leaf call safe' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 20 then,
        'gen leaf butif print' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 21 then,
        'gen leaf butif append' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 22 then,
        'gen leaf environment inrange' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 23 then,
        'gen leaf arguments inrange' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 24 then,
        'gen leaf deep arithmetic' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 25 then,
        'gen leaf treating print' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 26 then,
        'gen leaf treating grid' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 27 then,
        'gen leaf environment oob' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 28 then,
        'gen leaf rich condition' of indent and nested.
    If kind is 29 then,
        'gen leaf while' of indent and nested.
