(Excludes the three On-error kinds (3, 5, 10). Adjudicated 2026-08-18:
 this is not a parser bug but the termination rule working as
 documented - a period after an On-error handler closes only the
 HANDLER, because On error is itself a nesting clause (LANGUAGE.md rule
 1), so a block whose closer ends on a handler is never actually closed
 by it. Restricting the mandatory closer to non-On-error kinds is
 therefore not a workaround but correctness: the closer's period must
 close the block, and only a plain leaf's period does. The hand-reduced
 evidence lives in docs/findings/parser-reject/1/, which also led to
 vox bug #25. On-error groups remain freely available in non-closing
 positions.

 Also excludes 9's key iteration and 14's list iteration, and swaps 6
 (call) for 19 (the same three call shapes minus the grid variant) -
 same rule 1 as above, not a separate defect. `For each`/grid `each` is
 itself a nesting clause, so when one of these is the mandatory closer,
 its own period closes THAT clause, not the enclosing block the closer
 was supposed to close - note 6b's guarantee ("the closing leaf's
 period closes the block") only holds for a leaf that does not itself
 open a clause. 9 and 14 have no such-clause-free sibling so they are
 dropped outright; 6 does (19), so a call stays available here at only
 the cost of its grid quarter. Full coverage, grid included, remains
 available in every non-closing position - both here and in
 'gen statement's own remap (which this mirrors, for the same reason:
 that path picks leaves directly too, with no second call through this
 function, so it needs the identical substitution).

 T3 additions 20-24 (but-if print/append, environment/arguments inrange,
 deep arithmetic) join this pool - each is hand-verified safe as a sole
 closer, per its own function comment. 25-28 (treating, environment oob,
 rich condition) do not, for the same reasons kind 9/14/6-grid don't;
 29-30 (While/Repeat) aren't reachable outside the true top level at
 all, so they can never even be offered here; 31 (Break/Continue loop
 control) is loop-bearing and top-level-only for the same two reasons at
 once, so it is held out of this pool too - see its own comment.)

To 'gen any leaf' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
