    If pick is 1 then,
        Set kind to 1.
    If pick is 2 then,
        Set kind to 2.
    If pick is 3 then,
        Set kind to 4.
    If pick is 4 then,
        Set kind to 7.
    If pick is 5 then,
        Set kind to 8.
    If pick is 6 then,
        Set kind to 11.
    If pick is 7 then,
        Set kind to 12.
    If pick is 8 then,
        Set kind to 13.
    If pick is 9 then,
        Set kind to 15.
    If pick is 10 then,
        Set kind to 19.
    If pick is 11 then,
        Set kind to 20.
    If pick is 12 then,
        Set kind to 21.
    If pick is 13 then,
        Set kind to 22.
    If pick is 14 then,
        Set kind to 23.
    If pick is 15 then,
        Set kind to 24.
    'gen dispatch leaf' of kind and indent and nested.

(A block's body always ends with a plain leaf statement (note 6b): the
 leaf's own period is what closes the block, whether or not the body
 also contains a nested loop or if before it. That closing leaf must
 never be an On-error kind - see the note on 'gen statement's
 'avoid onerror' parameter for why - so this always passes true.)

To 'gen body' with a number called depth and a text called indent.
    a boolean called 'block started' is 'gen statement' of depth and indent and true.
    If 'block started' then,
        'gen any leaf' of indent and true.

(depth is a precomputed, decrementing nesting budget (language note 3):
 3 at the true top-level statement stream, capped at 0. new-var (kind
 18) only grows the globally-referenceable v-pool at depth 3, the one
 place a declaration is unconditionally, permanently in scope; nested
 declarations inside gen's leaf kinds stay local to their own
 statement instead. Returns whether this call opened an unclosed
 block (loop/if), so 'gen body' knows whether it must still append a
 mandatory closer.

 'avoid onerror': true whenever this call's chosen leaf might become
 the LAST thing that closes an enclosing block (called from 'gen
 body') - a clause (If or For, at any depth, including the true top
 level) that closes on an On-error handler mis-parses the compiler's
 clause-close bookkeeping if the very next statement also touches
 On-error, corrupting everything after it (hand-reduced against
 0.4.2, matching the adjacency documented on 'gen any leaf'). Passing
 false (only 'gen program's true top-level call does) allows the
 three On-error leaf kinds through undisturbed, since a plain
 top-level statement never closes a clause.

 Kind space: 0-15 are leaves (see 'gen dispatch leaf'), 16 is a loop,
 17 is an if, 18 grows the v-pool, 19 is a remap-only target. T3 adds
 20-24 (always-safe leaves), 25-28 (leaves that need the same
 avoid-onerror remap as 3/5/6/9/10/14, for the reasons given on each
 leaf's own comment), and 29-30 (While/Repeat, top-level only - see
 'gen leaf while's comment for why). D4 adds 31 (Break/Continue loop
 control, top-level only - same rule as 29/30, see 'gen leaf loop
 control's comment). depth 3 draws from the full range;
 depth 2 and 1 drop new-var (nesting must not permanently grow scope)
 and 29-31 (their termination guarantee depends on being an independent,
 self-closing top-level sentence, which nested positions are not); depth
 0 drops loop/if and every T3 addition too, leaves 0-15 only (nesting
 budget exhausted - kept to the pre-T3 range rather than widened, since
 the deepest level is the lowest-value place to add more surface).

 31-38 and 39-42 join 29-30 in the top-level-only band, since depth 2/1
 stop at 28 and depth 0 at 15. 39-42 are the format-string leaves
 (specifiers, expressions, values, every type); only 42 opens a clause -
 the If/Otherwise its formatted-time assertion needs - so only 42 joins
 the remap below. The others are declarations and Prints throughout and
 close a block correctly on their own period. Every remap entry for a
 kind above 28 is defensive rather than load-bearing: avoid_onerror is
 only ever true below 'gen body', which recurses at depth 2 or lower,
 where those kinds cannot be drawn in the first place. They are written
 anyway so the guarantee does not have to hold forever for the remap to
 stay correct.)

To 'gen statement' with a number called depth and a text called indent and a boolean called 'avoid onerror'.
    a number called kind is 0.
    If depth is equal to 3 then,
        (0..42: the four format leaves are 39-42. The file-write leaf
         is parked at 99, outside the draw, so no number in this range
         dispatches to nothing.
         45..52 are drawn as one contiguous span with the rest and then
         shifted into the collections surface's reserved 140-147 band -
         the eight `value` leaves. The shift keeps a single flat draw
         (so the existing kinds' odds move by exactly the eight new
         ones' share, and no seed's leaf choice depends on where the
         band happens to sit) while leaving the band numbers themselves
         where the reservation table put them, out of the way of the
         other surfaces' parallel work.)
        Set kind to 'rng below' of 53.
        If kind is greater than 44 then,
            Set kind to kind add 95.
    If depth is equal to 2 then,
        Set kind to 'rng below' of 29.
    If depth is equal to 1 then,
        Set kind to 'rng below' of 29.
    If depth is equal to 0 then,
        Set kind to 'rng below' of 16.
    (kind 18 (new-var) is only in the depth-2/1 range's span because 29
     needs a single contiguous rng_below upper bound to cover 20-28 too;
     depth 3's own draw is what makes 18 meaningful, so a depth-2/1 hit
     on it is neutralized here rather than left to grow the permanent
     v-pool from a conditionally-executed nested branch.)
    If kind is 18 and depth is equal to 2 then,
        Set kind to 1.
    If kind is 18 and depth is equal to 1 then,
        Set kind to 1.
    a boolean called 'is nested' is true.
    If depth is equal to 3 then,
        Set 'is nested' to false.
    If kind is 16 then,
        'gen loop' of depth and indent,
        Return a boolean, true.
    If kind is 17 then,
        'gen if' of depth and indent,
        Return a boolean, true.
    If kind is 18 then,
        a number called ignored is 'gen new var',
        Return a boolean, false.
    a number called 'safe kind' is kind.
    (Pre-existing generator defect, found and fixed while extending this
     function: the innermost 'If kind is 10' only closes itself with one
     period (rule 1), so without a second one here the outer 'If avoid
     onerror' clause was never closed and silently absorbed the dispatch
     call and the Return below into its own conditional body. At the
     true top level avoid_onerror is false, so that swallowed tail never
     ran - every plain top-level leaf silently emitted nothing, the
     whole time, for every seed. It went unnoticed because loop/if
     bodies recurse through the separately-closed avoid_onerror=true
     path below 'gen body', which still produced visible leaf output,
     making a program look populated even with the true top level
     silently empty. The double period closes both the inner-if and the
     outer avoid-onerror clause, so the dispatch call actually runs
     unconditionally, as the indentation always implied it should.)
    (Same remap for the three For-each-bearing kinds (6's grid variant,
     9, 14) and the same reason as the On-error trio above: whenever
     this leaf might be the one whose period closes an enclosing block
     (avoid_onerror true), a `For each`/grid `each` clause inside it is
     itself a nesting clause (LANGUAGE.md rule 1), so its period closes
     THAT, not the enclosing block - the enclosing If or loop is then
     one level further open than the rest of this generator assumes,
     and whatever comes next is silently declared inside it instead of
     at the level intended. Not a compiler defect (see 'gen if's own
     comment) - the compiler is right that a declaration only inside a
     single If branch is out of scope after it; the fix is simply never
     letting one of these be the block's closer. 9 and 14 have no safe
     sibling, so they fall back to 1 (assign); 6 does have one - 19 is
     'gen leaf call' minus the grid variant - so a call as closer keeps
     three quarters of its coverage instead of losing all of it.

     Every branch below tests the ORIGINAL kind, never 'safe kind' -
     these are independent substitutions, not a chain. kind 10 (map
     oob) used to hop to 9 (map inrange) as its On-error remap, back
     when 9 was itself a safe closer; now that 9 is unsafe too, 10 has
     to be remapped directly to 1, the same as 9 and 14 - routing it
     through 9 first would only test the untouched original `kind`
     (still 10) against "kind is 9", never fire, and leave 'safe kind'
     sitting at 9, still For-each-bearing. Hand-reduced: this exact
     chaining mistake was live in an earlier version of this remap and
     produced seed 3's "Unknown map: m3" before it was caught.)
    If 'avoid onerror' then,
        If kind is 3 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 2.
        If kind is 5 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 4.
        If kind is 10 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 9 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 14 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 6 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 19.
        If kind is 25 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 26 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 27 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 28 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        (Renumbered when main's `gen leaf loop control` claimed kind 31 in
         a merge: these three are the same three leaves as before - deep
         grid, cast-and-break, and timer-and-clock - each of which opens
         a For-each clause and therefore cannot be a block's closer.
         Main's loop control is NOT here because it emits through
         'gen join lines' as a single line, so its own terminator closes
         what it opened.)
        If kind is 32 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 35 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 37 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        (42 is 'gen leaf format types', which opens a clause of its own —
         carried over from the format-coverage branch's own remap.)
        If kind is 42 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        (140-147, the `value` leaves. Defensive in the same sense as the
         entries above: avoid_onerror is only ever true below 'gen body',
         which recurses at depth 2 or lower, and the band is drawn at
         depth 3 alone - so these can never be reached here. Written
         anyway, because every one of them either defines a function or
         ends on an `If ... Exit 95.` assertion, and both would leave an
         enclosing block open if the guarantee ever moved.)
        If kind is greater than 139 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1..
    'gen dispatch leaf' of 'safe kind' and indent and 'is nested'.
    Return a boolean, false.

To 'gen emit prelude functions'.
    a number called c1 is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called fn1 is "To f1.\n    Return a number, {c1}.\n\n".
    'gen emit' of fn1.
    a number called c2 is 'rng below' of 50 add 1.
    a text called fn2 is "To f2 with a number called p1.\n    a number called r is p1 add {c2}.\n    Return a number, r.\n\n".
    'gen emit' of fn2.
    a text called fn3 is "To f3 with a number called p1 and a number called p2.\n    a number called r is p1 add p2.\n    Return a number, r.\n\n".
    'gen emit' of fn3.
    (f4 takes a TEXT, which f1/f2/f3 between them never did - so before
     it there was no function in a generated program that a format
     string could be passed to, and item 3's "passing one to a function"
     was unreachable. It prints its argument rather than returning it,
     because the sink under test is the ARGUMENT position: a format
     string materializes at the call site, and what has to survive is
     the value the callee receives.)
    a text called fn4 is "To f4 with a text called p1.\n    Print \"f4 {{p1}}\".\n\n".
    'gen emit' of fn4.

(Section ordering. Plan 324 Part A, and Josj's own words on 2026-08-20:
 "globals can't be defined before functions". They could not: the
 skeleton was a fixed template - things, methods, functions, globals,
 statements - identical across all 40 programs measured. A generator
 that only ever emits one ordering is structurally incapable of finding
 an order-dependent defect, which is exactly what compiler bug #28 was
 (a conditional declaration BEFORE a top-level one segfaults; the same
 two declarations in the opposite order are fine).

 What may move, and what may not, established by hand against 0.4.6
 rather than assumed:

 - The flag schema and `Parse flags.` stay first. A flag variable used
   before the parse point is a compile-time error, and the statement
   stream reads flags.
 - Things and their methods stay next: a method names its owner type,
   and the statement stream instantiates them.
 - Functions, grid sinks, the flag reader and the globals may appear in
   any order among themselves. Vox permits a forward reference from a
   function body to a global declared BELOW it - verified: a function
   that increments a global declared four lines later prints 42 - so
   putting globals last is legal, and putting them first (what Josj
   asked for) equally so.
 - Globals stay ahead of the statement stream: a top-level statement
   reading a name declared later is use-before-declaration, which is a
   different question from the function case above.

 Six orderings rather than a general shuffle: it is enough to clear plan
 324's acceptance bar (at least four distinct orderings across 100
 seeds), and every one of the six is legal by inspection, which a
 generated permutation would not be. The globals count varies 0..3 as
 well, so the degenerate "no globals at all" shape is reachable - the
 statement stream already handles an empty pool ('gen var ref' returns
 the literal 0).)

(One global minimum, not zero. The truly degenerate no-globals shape is
 reachable by the skeleton but NOT yet by the statement stream: several
 leaves use 'gen var ref' as an assignment TARGET, and with an empty
 pool that helper returns the literal "0", producing `Set 0 to 0 times
 0.` - invalid Vox, which the invariant forbids. Caught by seed 300019
 in the first 300-seed campaign after ordering landed (276/300
 compiled; before ordering it was 250/250).

 The right fix is to teach the assigning leaves to declare a variable
 when the pool is empty, which makes the no-globals program legal
 instead of merely unreachable. Until then the count starts at one, so
 the ordering variation - which is what this change is for - is kept
 and the invalid shape is not emitted. Recorded rather than papered
 over: a generator that emits illegal Vox manufactures false findings,
 which is the one thing this repo must never do.)

To 'gen emit globals block'.
    a number called 'how many' is 'rng below' of 4.
    a number called made is 0.
    While made is less than 'how many',
        a number called ignored is 'gen new var',
        Set made to made add 1.

(Six blocks, in a seeded order. Josj, 2026-08-20, reading forty
 generated programs: "I wasn't expecting to see such well behaved
 programs." He was right, and the measurement agreed with him — nine
 distinct orderings across those forty, and yet EVERY one opened
 `FLAG-THING-...`, because the flag schema and the things were pinned
 to the front. The first thirty lines of every program were therefore
 identical in shape, which is exactly the part a human reads first.

 Both pins were mine, not the language's, and both were wrong. Verified
 by hand against 0.4.7: a thing may be defined after a function that
 does not use it, and the flag schema may follow things and functions
 provided it still precedes `Parse flags.` and every flag read.

 What genuinely constrains the order, and all that does:
 - the flag block ends with `Parse flags.`, and the statement stream
   reads flags, so the flag block must precede the statements — which
   it does by construction, since all six blocks precede them;
 - a thing's methods name its owner type, so things and methods travel
   as one block rather than separately;
 - globals must precede the statement stream, since a top-level
   statement reading a name declared later is use-before-declaration.
   A FUNCTION may still reference a global declared below it — Vox
   permits that forward reference, and it is now genuinely exercised.

 Rotating six blocks rather than enumerating permutations: 720 hand-
 written orderings would be absurd, and a rotation plus an independent
 swap gives plenty of distinct openings while every ordering stays
 legal by inspection, which a generated permutation would not.)

To 'gen emit one block' with a number called which.
    If which is 0 then,
        'gen emit prelude flags'.
    If which is 1 then,
        'gen emit things block'.
    If which is 2 then,
        'gen emit prelude functions'.
    If which is 3 then,
        'gen emit prelude grid sinks'.
    If which is 4 then,
        'gen emit prelude flag reader'.
    If which is 5 then,
        'gen emit globals block'.

To 'gen emit ordered blocks'.
    a number called 'the rotation' is 'rng below' of 6.
    a number called 'the swap from' is 'rng below' of 6.
    a number called 'the swap to' is 'rng below' of 6.
    a number called i is 0.
    While i is less than 6,
        a number called 'the slot' is i add 'the rotation',
        Set 'the slot' to 'the slot' modulo 6,
        (The swap writes to a SEPARATE name because both tests must see
         the ORIGINAL slot. Chaining them on one variable collapses the
         pair: slot==from becomes to, then the second test sees `to` and
         sends it straight back to from — so from and to both emit the
         same block and one block never emits at all. That produced
         `label f1 inconsistently redefined` from nasm, 58 of 60
         programs failing to assemble.)
        a number called 'the mapped slot' is 'the slot',
        If 'the slot' is 'the swap from' then,
            Set 'the mapped slot' to 'the swap to'.
        If 'the slot' is 'the swap to' then,
            Set 'the mapped slot' to 'the swap from'.
        'gen emit one block' of 'the mapped slot',
        Set i to i add 1.

To 'gen program' with a number called seed and a number called budget.
    clear gen_out.
    Set gen_vars to 0.
    Set gen_bufs to 0.
    Set gen_lists to 0.
    Set gen_instances to 0.
    Set gen_args to 0.
    Set gen_maps to 0.
    Set gen_values to 0.
    Set gen_floats to 0.
    Set gen_texts to 0.
    Set gen_grid to 0.
    Set gen_whiles to 0.
    Set gen_repeats to 0.
    Set gen_envs to 0.
    Set gen_flags to 0.
    'rng seed' of seed.
    (The value leaves spend names out of a vocabulary on a per-program
     cycle whose start and stride are DRAWN, so the reset has to sit
     after the reseed, not with the plain counters above it: drawn from
     the previous program's leftover stream, two programs generated in
     one process stop being byte-identical for the same seed - which is
     exactly what 040_gen's `reproducible` line caught.)
    'gen reset value names'.
    Set gen_grid_depth to 'rng below' of 15 add 3.
    Set gen_flags to 1.
    'gen build argv'.
    'gen build input'.
    Set gen_reads_stdin to false.
    Set gen_does_file_io to false.
    Set gen_stdin_reads to 0.
    Set gen_file_ios to 0.
    (Drawn through a PRIME modulus, not 'rng below' of 2. This LCG has
     correlated low bits — the hazard is documented above 'gen leaf loop
     control', and I walked straight into it anyway. Two consecutive
     `'rng below' of 2` draws are anti-correlated: measured over 150
     programs, two supposedly even coin flips came out 75% and 25%, one
     high exactly where the other was low.
     `r modulo 13` depends on the whole of r rather than its bottom bit,
     so consecutive draws are independent. Thresholds of 6 and 7 give
     roughly 46% and 54%.)
    a number called 'the file choice' is 'rng below' of 13.
    If 'the file choice' is less than 6 then,
        Set gen_does_file_io to true.
    a number called 'the stdin choice' is 'rng below' of 13.
    If 'the stdin choice' is less than 7 then,
        Set gen_reads_stdin to true.
    Set gen_grid_calls to 0.
    Set gen_reader_calls to 0.
    (Branch form, not `Set x to <comparison>` — a comparison cannot be
     assigned or used to initialise in Vox, which the compiler rejects
     with "Expected a statement, got Is". Roughly two programs in three
     commit to each.)
    a number called 'the grid choice' is 'rng below' of 3.
    Set gen_uses_grid to false.
    If 'the grid choice' is not 0 then,
        Set gen_uses_grid to true.
    a number called 'the reader choice' is 'rng below' of 3.
    Set gen_uses_flag_reader to false.
    If 'the reader choice' is not 0 then,
        Set gen_uses_flag_reader to true.
    'gen emit ordered blocks'.
    a number called i is 0.
    While i is less than budget,
        a boolean called 'top opened' is 'gen statement' of 3 and "" and false,
        Set i to i add 1.
    (Guarantee the definitions earn their place: if the program committed
     to a block but the statement stream never happened to call it, emit
     one call now, at the true top level where it is always legal.)
    (Flat conditions, not nested Ifs. A period closes only the INNERMOST
     open clause, so `If a then, If b then, <one statement>.` closes the
     inner If and leaves the OUTER one open to swallow whatever follows —
     which is why the grid guarantee silently never fired while the
     reader's did: the reader's inner body was comma-joined and happened
     to close both.)
    If gen_reads_stdin and gen_stdin_reads is 0 then,
        'gen leaf stdin read' of "" and false.
    If gen_does_file_io and gen_file_ios is 0 then,
        'gen leaf file round trip' of "" and false.
    If gen_uses_grid and gen_grid_calls is 0 then,
        'gen leaf deep grid' of "" and false.
    If gen_uses_flag_reader and gen_reader_calls is 0 then,
        a text called 'the forced reader call' is "'read flags {gen_flags}'.\n",
        'gen emit' of 'the forced reader call'.
    (91-94 are RESERVED for the argv assertions and must never be drawn
     as an ordinary exit code, or a program that passed its assertions
     could exit 92 by chance and be reported as having failed one. The
     draw skips the reserved band rather than rejecting and redrawing,
     so the distribution stays flat and deterministic.
     256 - 4 = 252 possible codes, and anything at or above 91 is
     shifted up by four.)
    a number called 'the raw code' is 'rng below' of 252.
    a number called code is 'the raw code'.
    If 'the raw code' is greater than 90 then,
        Set code to 'the raw code' add 4.
    a text called tail is "Exit {code}.\n".
    'gen emit' of tail.

(256, not 101: the shell-era cap is gone, and codes above 128 are the
 ones a shell-reported status would have misclassified as signal deaths -
 the native harness must prove it tells them apart, so the generator must
 produce them.)

To 'gen text'.
    a text called t is "{gen_out}".
    Return a text, t.

To 'gen write' with a text called path.
    open a file for writing called gf at path.
    Write gen_out to gf.
    Close gf.

(Byte-scan a needle over gen_out - used by tests to assert the
 generator actually produced a given construct.)

To 'gen text matches at' with a number called start and a buffer called needle and a number called 'needle length'.
    a number called offset is 0.
    a boolean called 'bytes match' is true.
    While offset is less than 'needle length',
        a number called 'hay pos' is start add offset,
        a number called 'hay byte' is byte 'hay pos' of gen_out,
        a number called 'needle pos' is offset add 1,
        a number called 'needle byte' is byte 'needle pos' of needle,
        If 'hay byte' is not 'needle byte' then,
            Set 'bytes match' to false.
        Set offset to offset add 1.
    Return a boolean, 'bytes match'.

To 'gen text contains' with a text called needle.
    a buffer called 'needle buf' is "{needle}".
    a number called 'haystack length' is gen_out's length.
    a number called 'needle length' is 'needle buf's length.
    a boolean called found is false.
    a number called 'last start' is 'haystack length' minus 'needle length' add 1.
    a number called 'scan start' is 1.
    While 'scan start' is less than or equal to 'last start',
        a boolean called 'match here' is 'gen text matches at' of 'scan start' and 'needle buf' and 'needle length',
        If 'match here' then,
            Set found to true.
        Set 'scan start' to 'scan start' add 1.
    Return a boolean, found.
